This study focuses on dyadic dynamics, bidirectional learning, and coping as an interactional, episodic, and adaptive process in DBER postdoc-faculty collaborations. The central argument is that agency development is a property of the interaction system — not the individual postdoc — and that not all tensions between postdocs and supervisors lead to negative outcomes. Drawing on interview data from postdoc-faculty dyads, we identify four patterns of bidirectional learning: learning that the position requires real-time co-development rather than execution of a pre-built plan; that positional liminality is an asset rather than a limitation; that structure enables freedom and that postdocs change faculty careers; and that change leadership requires coalition-building, not just good curriculum. Findings directly challenge models that locate agency development inside the individual and carry implications for program design, faculty supervisor development, position scoping, and evaluation of instructional change outcomes.